Arm a quick suggestion while an Android keyboard composes a word.

The suggest model follows the caret only while no composition is open.
Its cursor listener returns early while the editor composes, because on
a desktop what a composition holds is a dead key's accent or an input
method's reading, text that is about to become something else, and a
quick suggestion is armed from that listener, by a caret that typing
moves to the right. An Android keyboard composes whole words: from the
first letter of a word to the space after it, every key is an update of
one open composition. Nothing was armed while a word was typed, and a
list came only when the composition ended: at the space, where no word
is left to complete, or when the person put the keyboard away, tapped
the editor or left the app, each of which ends the composition with the
word still before the caret. On an API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4 and
WebView 134, alp typed in a JavaScript file that declares alphabet and
alphanumeric showed no list, and Back, which puts the keyboard away,
showed both names. With Gboard's suggestion strip turned off the
keyboard composes nothing, and the list came as on a desktop on the same
emulator without this patch. Patch 0053 refilters a list that is open
while a keyboard composes; nothing opened one.

On Android the cursor listener now hands a caret move made during a
composition to the code it runs with none (_onCursorChange) when typing
made the move (an empty selection, reason NotSet, source keyboard or
deleteLeft, which is the test the listener already made for 0025) and
the word before the caret is text as it was typed: it ends in a letter
that is not a modifier letter, a digit, an underscore or a dollar sign,
with any combining marks after it, and holds no character of Han,
Hiragana, Katakana, Hangul or Bopomofo and no full-width form.
Everything after that is the code a desktop runs: the quick suggest
delay, editor.quickSuggestions and the kind of token at the caret, the
test for a word at the caret, and with a list open a typed letter arms
nothing, the list being refiltered by 0053. A caret that anything else
moves during a composition arms and cancels nothing, as before, so a
list stays open through the selections a keyboard sets by itself; its
place is noted, so that the next typed move is compared with where the
caret was. Left at the place of the last followed move, a Backspace over
an apostrophe read as a move to the right and armed a suggestion, and a
letter typed after the keyboard had moved the caret back armed none.
Other platforms return early as before.

The scripts left out are the ones a keyboard converts before it commits:
kana and kanji, the jamo of a Hangul syllable, bopomofo. The word before
the caret is then a reading that a conversion is about to replace.
Gboard's Japanese keyboard holds the consonant of a syllable it has not
finished as a full-width letter (k typed for ka is U+FF4B until the a
arrives, recorded on the same emulator), which is why full-width forms
are left out as well. A lone accent of a dead key does not pass the
first test: most are symbols, and the ones Android shows as modifier
letters (a circumflex, a caron and a grave are U+02C6, U+02C7 and
U+02CB) are why modifier letters do not count, which leaves out a
bopomofo tone and the kana length mark as well. A word that ends in a
combining mark after its letter, as a Thai syllable with a tone or a
letter followed by a combining accent does, arms like one that ends in
the letter; a lone mark, one after a modifier letter and one after a
converted script arm nothing. A Latin word
typed straight after a character of those scripts, with no space, arms
nothing where the language's word pattern takes the two as one word.

With the patch built into the server, on that emulator: with alp before
the caret, h showed alphabet and alphanumeric, an brought the list down
to alphanumeric and Backspace brought both back; a tap on a row and the
key row's Tab each wrote the whole name once; Space closed the list; a
word taken from the keyboard's strip with the list open was written with
its space and closed the list; a period arrived inside the composition's
text and accepted nothing, and the list a trigger character opens
followed it. In a plain text file rel showed relay and relays from the
words of the file. The list is asked of the language's providers and
comes when they answer: in seven runs of Enter and then al it was up
half a second after the l in five, 1.7 s after it in one, and not yet up
after 1.2 s in one, where a Space typed next ended it. On Gboard's
Japanese keyboard k, ka, kere and an Enter that commits showed no list
at any step. With don typed, an apostrophe added and then deleted, a
build of this patch that did not note the apostrophe's move showed the
list again, some seconds late; this one showed none in 8 s, and the
letter typed after brought it.

What it does not change: Enter. Gboard sends Enter in the middle of an
English word as a line break inside the composition's text, so it makes
a new line and closes the list, as it did when no list was there; a
suggestion is accepted by a tap on its row or by the key row's Tab, and
by Enter only where the keyboard sends it as a key. What it costs: the
list now opens under or over the line while a word is typed, in every
file whose language arms quick suggestions, plain text included, as it
does on a desktop; editor.quickSuggestions turns it off. A pinyin
keyboard composes Latin letters that a pick replaces with characters,
which the test cannot tell from an English word: on the same emulator ke
typed on Gboard's pinyin keyboard in a text file that holds keeps showed
keeps while the candidates were offered, and the list closed when a
candidate was taken. An Enter that commits the letters as they are
leaves the list open and the next Enter accepts the suggestion; a build
without this patch does the same, with the list opening at the commit
(both measured). Where a language arms no suggestion in comments and
strings, which is the default, Chinese typed there shows none. A
keyboard that sends punctuation as a typed key and not inside the
composition accepts the focused suggestion at a commit character, as a
desktop does; Gboard 12.4 does not send it that way.

scripts/check-editcontext-ime.js holds its cases, over the patched
suggestModel.ts with the editor replaced by an adapter. Suggestions are
expected for a letter and the next one, for a letter with a diacritic, a
Cyrillic letter, a digit after a letter, an underscore and a dollar
sign, for an uppercase and a titlecase last letter, for a word that ends
in a combining mark after its letter, and for a letter typed after the
keyboard moved the caret back; each of those fails without the patch.
Nothing is expected off Android, for a deletion, for a Backspace over an
apostrophe, for a caret that typing did not move or a selection the
keyboard makes (an open list stays open and is not refiltered), for
kana, a Hangul syllable, a Han character, bopomofo and a full-width
letter, alone or beside Latin letters, for a lone accent or another
modifier letter, and for a lone mark, one after a modifier letter and
one after a converted script; each of those fails when the test it
stands for is removed. One case, a typed letter with a list open, holds
on every tree and pins what the patch must not change. Not run on a
device: a Korean keyboard, a keyboard that composes with dead keys, and
Gboard versions other than 12.4.

With the patch built into the server, on an API 33 emulator with Gboard
12.4 and Thai added, which is composed word by word (the updates read ก,
then กั, then กัน and a space): a plain text file held the word กัน and
a new line; ก was typed and the list opened with กัน; Escape closed it;
the key of the mark ั (U+0E31, the composition now กั) opened nothing on
the server published before this patch and opened the list with กัน with
it.

